    "problem_statement": "Connection.trailing_data returns incorrect buffer data after protocol switch\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen using `Connection.trailing_data` after a protocol switch, the returned buffer data is truncated and the closed status is inverted. This affects protocol switching scenarios where you need to access the remaining data in the receive buffer.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport h11\n\n# Create a server connection\nconn = h11.Connection(h11.SERVER)\n\n# Simulate receiving a CONNECT request with trailing data\nconn.receive_data(b'CONNECT example.com:443 HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: foo\\r\\nContent-Length: 1\\r\\n\\r\\n1')\n\n# Process the request\nrequest = conn.next_event()\nbody = conn.next_event()\nend = conn.next_event()\n\n# Send acceptance response to switch protocols\nconn.send(h11.Response(status_code=200, headers=[]))\n\n# Add some trailing data after protocol switch\nconn.receive_data(b'123456')\n\n# Check trailing data - this should return the full buffer\nbuffer, closed = conn.trailing_data\nprint(f\"Buffer: {buffer}\")\nprint(f\"Closed: {closed}\")\n\n# Expected: Buffer should be b'123456', closed should be False\n# Actual: Buffer is truncated and closed status is wrong\n```\n\nThe `trailing_data` property should return the complete buffer contents and correct closed status, but currently returns truncated data with inverted closed flag."
